          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is whey concentrate or isolate better for building muscle?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Neither builds more muscle at the same protein dose. Their amino acid profiles are nearly identical, and a 25 g serving of either supplies roughly 2.5 g of leucine. Isolate simply carries less lactose, fat and carbohydrate per scoop. Choose isolate if lactose bothers you or you track macros closely, and save money with concentrate or a blend if not."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much whey protein should I take to gain muscle?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Total daily protein matters most. Research suggests around 1.6 g per kilogram of body weight a day maximizes gains for most lifters, with an upper range near 2.2 g. One scoop of 20 to 30 g covers a meal's worth for most people. Use whey to close the gap between what you eat and your target, not as a replacement for meals."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is grass-fed whey worth the extra cost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if sourcing matters to you. Grass-fed claims describe how the cows were raised, and in the US there is no single mandatory standard behind them. The nutritional benefits of grass-fed milk fat barely survive into a powder that is mostly protein. Grass-fed whey builds the same muscle as conventional whey at the same dose, so treat it as a values choice."
          }
        },
